  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligated $13B in contracts in FY2026 to date. The agency recorded 7,317 awards, with an average award size of $1.8M. Obligations fell 17.9% year over year. Fiscal trends show a pullback from the recent peak of $16.1B in FY23, with obligations of $15B in FY21, $15.2B in FY22, $16.1B in FY23, $15B in FY24, $15.8B in FY25, and $13B in FY26. Research and development is the single largest category: Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) totaled $4.9B in FY2026 to date. NASA also spent $701M on Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Biotechnology) and $409M on Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences. Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing accounted for $2.4B. CALIFORNIA INSTITUTE OF TECHNOLOGY received $1.9B and THE BOEING COMPANY received $1B in FY2026 to date, followed by BLUE ORIGIN, LLC at $735M and THE JOHNS HOPKINS UNIVERSITY APPLIED PHYSICS LABORATORY LLC at $485M.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How have NASA's contract obligations changed year over year?",
        "body": "The National Aeronautics and Space Administration's obligations fell 17.9% year over year. Obligations by fiscal year were $15B in FY21, $15.2B in FY22, $16.1B in FY23, $15B in FY24, $15.8B in FY25, and $13B in FY26. The FY2026 to date total of $13B reflects partial current-year obligations."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors dominate NASA contracting?",
        "body": "Research and development categories dominate NASA contracting in FY2026 to date, with $4.9B for Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology), $701M for Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Biotechnology), and $409M for Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences. Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing accounted for $2.4B. CALIFORNIA INSTITUTE OF TECHNOLOGY received $1.9B and THE BOEING COMPANY received $1B, followed by BLUE ORIGIN, LLC at $735M and THE JOHNS HOPKINS UNIVERSITY APPLIED PHYSICS LABORATORY LLC at $485M."
      }
    ]
